Document Hierarchy & Single Source of Truth (Mandatory)
Apply on every decision and every edit.

The authoritative and only statement of these rules is docs/SPEC.md §0 — the hierarchy, the
conflict resolution, the anti-duplication rules and the change protocol all live there. Read
it before acting. Do not restate any of it here or anywhere else; §0's own Forbidden list
bans exactly that.

Project Context
Informatics student portfolio project for agriculture-tech job applications.
Student must explain every part of the codebase in interviews.
Optimize for clarity and teachability, not cleverness.

Interview-First Coding
- Prefer simple, readable code the student can walk through on a whiteboard.
- Prefer clear names over short names.
- Prefer straightforward control flow over clever abstractions.
- Do not use advanced patterns unless they already exist in the repo and are necessary.
- Do not add layers for “enterprise best practice” (extra interfaces, factories, event buses, DI frameworks beyond Spring’s defaults).
- Keep methods short and purpose-obvious.
- Do not add new languages, frameworks, brokers, or databases beyond modules listed in ARCHITECTURE.md.

Working Rules
- Make the smallest change that completes the requested task.
- Match existing package style (com.mariolagger.*, cased per GLOSSARY.md §1), DTOs, and constructor injection.
- Do not implement anything listed under SPEC.md §8 Future work, or otherwise out of scope.
- If unsure, choose the option easier to explain in an interview.
- If any requirement, detail, decision, edge case, or implementation choice is not fully understood, ask multiple-choice questions covering every uncertainty before making any changes. Prefer the maximum number of questions needed; more questions are better than assumptions. Do not implement until all answers are received and understanding is complete.

Response Style
Prefer brevity. Avoid unnecessary explanations, restatements, or filler unless the user explicitly asks for more detail.